5815	Solanum aviculare	2021-10-20 17:09:27	iNaturalist research-grade observations	Not in a reserve	-33.913889	151.1225	\N	\N	\N	[All these qualities can be observed within the photographs.] Firstly, this plant has distinct qualities of the family Solanaceae; woody shrub, purple flowers with 5 petals and berries as fruit. Secondly, it has distinct qualities of the genus Solanum; pinnatisect leaves, firm berries and stellate, purple, 5 lobed flowers in a cymose/panicle-like inflorescence (Fig 5). Thirdly, it has distinct qualities of the species Solanum aviculare; non-climbing, erect, light violet corolla, entire margins with leaf length at 2-8 times as its width, leaves are either lobed or entire with a stalk, egg-shaped berries and mostly glabrous on all surfaces. This species is commonly confused with Solanum laciniatum. However, the above pictures show no notches in the corolla lobes. It is also confused with Solanum vescum which unlike S. aviculare has a spherical shaped berry and stalkless leaves. Unfortunately, the berries aren’t ripe, and the final colour isn’t known to be able to effectively use the key. Hence the focus is on the berries shape and leaves. Extra info: The common name, Kangaroo apple is inspired by the shape of the leaves which is thought to resemble a kangaroo’s foot. Whilst the scientific name ‘aviculare’ refers to little bird in Latin after the wing-like appendages on the leaves. References include Field Guide to the Native Plants by Les Robinson (2003) and PlantNET. Photographed during the day.	2021	10	20	\N	\N	\N		faene	2021-10-24T17:19:59Z	\N	HUMAN_OBSERVATION	\N	faene	CB Council
