5884	Burchardia umbellata	2024-10-04 13:09:00	iNaturalist research-grade observations	Not in a reserve	-33.973	151.017	\N	\N	\N	(Monocot) The number of flower bases is 6, indicating that the plant is a monocot. The flowers are whorled and arranged regularly, strictly related to the number of flower bases. The flowers are in the same perianth, showing strong characteristics of Liliales. After several identifications with plant identification software, it is almost certain that it is Colchicumaceae, a family that has recently branched from Liliaceae. Since there are no leaves, it is impossible to search through the key table of Colchicumaceae in plantnet, and Robinson Book does not even record this family (or maybe I can't find it), so we used the results of plant identification software to further search and found that Genus Burchardia is characterized by perennial herbs, smooth and hairless, with bulbs with papery scales, and tuberous roots. The leaves are mostly basal, 1 to 5, and folded. The scape is simple or has 1 to 4 branches, 1 to 4 bracts, and the inflorescence is umbrella-shaped, with up to 20 flowers or single flowers. The flowers are bisexual, with 6 tepals arranged in a whorl, similar and independent. There are 6 stamens, with anthers dorsifixed and split longitudinally, spreading outward. The ovary is superior, 3-chambered, with multiple ovules in each chamber, and the style is short and 3-lobed. The fruit is a capsule, with angular, brown seeds. In addition to these descriptions, the illustrations and pictures provided by plantnet also provide some hints. This information is almost completely consistent with our plant, with no discrepancies. So we think it belongs to the genus burchardia. Finally, since the only species of genus burchardia in NSW is Burchardia umbellata, we infer that this is Burchardia umbellata. This is also consistent with the results of the plant identification software.	2024	10	4	\N	\N	\N		tianzhou	2024-10-09T10:56:52Z	\N	HUMAN_OBSERVATION	\N	tianzhou	CB Council
