6033	Tradescantia fluminensis	2021-10-20 17:40:27	iNaturalist research-grade observations	Not in a reserve	-33.914872	151.128108	\N	\N	\N	[All these qualities can be observed within the photographs.] Firstly, this plant has distinct qualities of the family Commelinaceae; herbaceous monocot with conspicuous flowers in parts of 3 (6 tepals in 2 whorls of 3), a superior ovary and parallel veins in leaves. Secondly, it has distinct qualities of the genus Tradescantia; multiple pedicellate flower clusters enclosed with 2 large leafy bracts. Thirdly, it has distinct qualities of the species Tradescantia fluminensis; the dark green top surface of the leaves, the lighter green underside of the leaves, 3 free white inner tepals, 3 green outer tepals, 6 yellow anthers on filaments tufted with white hairs and no occurrence of fruit. It is unlikely to be the species Tradescantia zebrina which has pink tepals and purple on the underside of its leaves. Extra info: While amazing for groundcover, this plant is not ideal for native species conservation. The competitiveness of this plant has been incredibly damaging to our native habitats and plants. References include Field Guide to the Native Plants by Les Robinson (2003) and PlantNET. Photographed during the day.	2021	10	20	\N	\N	\N		faene	2021-10-24T19:39:31Z	\N	HUMAN_OBSERVATION	\N	faene	CB Council
