7422	Isopogon anemonifolius	2024-10-04 13:29:00	iNaturalist research-grade observations	Not in a reserve	-33.97	151.02	\N	\N	\N	Very distinctive flowers and flower heads, the flower axis is shortened into a coiled inflorescence, and the leaves are alternate. There are four tepals, which is a very typical feature of Proteaceae. The plant has dense globose or cylindrical inflorescences, the flowers are sessile and closely arranged, and the bracts become woody when they bear fruit. The inflorescence axis is usually less than 5 cm, the fruits are radiate or bilaterally symmetrical nuts, and the leaves are usually divided into multiple segments or a few are entire. The fruit falls off with the bracts, the nut is not significantly flattened, the flower head is inflorescence, and the perianth tube is not completely split to the base. It is consistent with the description of the plantnet, and it can be judged that it belongs to the genus Isopogon. The leaves are usually divided into flat or cylindrical segments. In rare cases, the leaves below the inflorescence are intact, and the leaves are linear and less than 5 mm wide. The width of the divided segments of the leaves exceeds 1 mm and often exceeds 3 mm; the plant grows in an upright or spreading posture. The perianth is yellow and hairless except for a tuft of short hairs at the top. The leaves are usually no more than 9 cm long, the length of the undivided part is roughly equal to the divided part, the width of the divided part is 3-5 mm, usually only the midrib is obvious or all veins are not obvious, and the end is not sharp; the perianth length is about 10-12 mm, consistent with the description of the plantnet, it can be judged to be Isopogon anemonifolius. In addition, based on the comparison with the Proteaceae column in Robinson Book, we think our view is correct. The plant does have a very characteristic spherical woody cone, which is a typical feature of the genus isopogon. The height of its shrub is not high, just over one meter, so we can also rule out isopogon anetbifolius, which is taller (to 3m). So it can only be Isopogon anemonifolius.	2024	10	4	\N	\N	\N		tianzhou	2024-10-09T10:49:57Z	\N	HUMAN_OBSERVATION	\N	tianzhou	CB Council
