8058	Lomandra hystrix	2020-10-20 10:49:00	iNaturalist research-grade observations	Not in a reserve	-33.927581	151.073246	\N	\N	\N	Clade: Monocots Family: Asparagaceae Genus: Lomandra Species: Lomandra hystrix (green mat-rush) - This species is in the Asparagaceae family as it is a shrub. Another trait which identifies this species in the Asparagaceae family is that it has long, hairless leaves with parallel venation. - This species is in the genus Lomandra as it contains long and narrow leaves which resemble blades. Furthermore, the base of the plant does not contain a stem. - Another feature which places this species in the Asparagaceae family is the fact that its flowers are arranged in whorled inflorescences. - Each inflorescence contains a cluster of spiky bracts. The flowers are very small and either white or cream coloured. - Lomandra hystrix is very similar to Lomandra longifolia, though I have excluded this possibility through the fact that the leaves of this species is pointed at the end, whereas the leaves of Lomandra longifolia end in a ‘W’ shape. - Yet another feature which distinguishes this species from L.longifolia is the fact that its flowers are in branched inflorescences (which are also open and loose), whereas the flowers of L.longifolia are not branched (as observed on Plantnet)	2020	10	20	\N	\N	\N		rsadia	2020-10-22T02:37:34Z	\N	HUMAN_OBSERVATION	\N	rsadia	CB Council
