8163	Kunzea ambigua	2021-10-14 14:04:05	iNaturalist research-grade observations	Not in a reserve	-33.920083	151.115541	\N	\N	\N	[All these qualities can be observed within the photographs.] Firstly, this plant has distinct qualities of the Myrtaceae family; woody shrub, simple entire leaves with oil glands, actinomorphic flowers with 5 petals, a half inferior ovary and numerous showy stamens. Secondly, it has distinct qualities of the Genus Kunzea; dehiscent fruit, flower buds without calyptras, free stamens that exceed petals in length, alternate leaves, thin leaves (<5mm wide), persistent sepals and flowers on leaf axils. Although Genus Kunzea and Leptospermum appear similar, the filaments of Kunzea exceed petal length unlike Leptospermum (filaments shorter than petals), hence why the plant is Genus Kunzea. Thirdly, it has distinct qualities of the species Kunzea ambigua; white flowers crowded near leaves, short pedicels and villous young stems. While Kunzea axillaris and Kunzea occidentalis are similar to K. ambigua, K. axillaris has far shorter leaves (3-5mm compared to 4-12mm) and K. occidentalis has more glabrous young stems. Extra Info: K. ambigua has a distinctly strong and enjoyable smell. Also, Les Robinson speculates that the term "ambigua" (meaning doubt in Latin) indicates the founders' original confusion with this species regarding its classification as Kunzea or Leptospermum. References include Field Guide to the Native Plants by Les Robinson (2003) and PlantNET. Photographed during the day.	2021	10	14	\N	\N	\N		faene	2021-10-24T14:28:37Z	\N	HUMAN_OBSERVATION	\N	faene	CB Council
