8534	Solanum aviculare	2023-10-02 12:35:10	iNaturalist research-grade observations	Not in a reserve	-33.978617	151.002128	\N	\N	\N	Using the description provided by PlantNET the plant would be: In the family Solanaceae due to it having simple pinnate leaves that are alternate, a calyx that is campanulate and a five lobed corolla that is stellate. In the genus Solanum because there appears to be 5 stamens that each have anthers that are 2-locular and basifixed. In image 4 the 5 stamens may be difficult to confirm, however the angle of the image and the symmetry of the plant could allow me to infer that the remaining 2 stamens were out of shot. For the presence of there being 2 locular and basifixed anthers, the apparent bulge and openings of the anthers in image 5 is shown through the white outline that shows that there are two parts. This also shows that the bulge that divides the two appears to align between the stamen beneath them, which can help confirm that there are 5 stamens in total through the number of 2-locular anthers. In the species Solanum aviculare because it’s an erect shrub, has a violet corolla, entire leaves, and lacks prickles. I’ve rejected Solanum linearifolium as an option as it is not glabrous. I’ve also rejected Solanum vescum because the size of the leaf is different with our plant not having a measurement of 5 to 15 cm long and 0.5 to 1.3 cm wide.	2023	10	2	\N	\N	\N		chatcastle	2023-10-14T05:38:32Z	\N	HUMAN_OBSERVATION	\N	chatcastle	CB Council
