9787	Dianella caerulea	2024-10-04 00:00:00	iNaturalist observations	Not in a reserve	-33.972	151.011	\N	\N	\N	(Monocot) Flower bases of 6, very typical parallel leaf veins, suggesting a monocotyledon. The synephridium suggests that it may be a plant of the order Liliaceae. However, after repeated and careful screening with plant identification software and against images of confirmed observations available on plant net and iNat, we believe that this may in fact be Dianella caerulea, commonly known as Blue flax-lily, in the family Asphodelaceae. very characteristic reflexed petals, very characteristic buds with yellow structures on the margins, racemes, purple oval berries, and overall a very recognisable flower. It is worth noting that this family does not appear to be recorded in the Robinson Book under "Monocots", so I think this family, and this species, may be of very good and unique identification value, and I hope you will draw attention to it. Our Alfalfa plants are herbaceous, with berries, anthers that do not curl after dehiscence, and stamens whose filaments are inflated and papillate at the tips. The flowers are strongly tubular and of mixed reddish-brown-orange colour. Anthers dehiscent through apical pores, filaments attached at or near the base of the anthers, anthers inactive. Leaf blades spreading, flowers borne in axils or not alone at apices; plants non-alpine types. Leaves mostly basal or reduced and bracteate along above-ground stems, flowers often borne on nearly leafless scapes or apical inflorescences. Underground stems are well developed, usually bulbs, corms, tubers or rhizomes. This agrees with Genus Dianella as described by plantnet. Our Alfalfa family has inflorescences that clearly exceed the height of the leaf blades; leaf margins and midveins are scabrous at least at the base of the leaf; and grows in non-marine environments. Plants yellowish green or greyish green, with anthers longer than the expanded part of the filaments; fruit more than 4 mm long, shorter than or equal to its width; leaf margins smooth or minutely spiny. Leaf sheaths folded, usually with distinct keel-like ridges on the lower surface. Vein spacing on the lower surface of the leaf blade greater than or equal to the width of the veins, without papillae; anthers yellow, yellow-brown or blue. Leaf sheaths cover more than half. A comparison using plantnet's key locates Dianella caerulea, which is consistent with the results obtained by our previous plant identification software, so we deduce that this monocotyledon is Dianella caerulea. We found this plant in the right hand direction on Burns Rd at Picnic Point on our way into Georges River National Park. Given that Blue flax-lily occurs in a wide range of habitats and is easy to grow, I think it makes sense that it shows off in that area.	2024	10	4	\N	\N	\N		\N	\N	\N	HUMAN_OBSERVATION	\N	tianzhou	CB Council
