10582	Mirbelia rubiifolia	2024-10-06 14:00:00	iNaturalist research-grade observations	Not in a reserve	-33.97	151.01	\N	\N	\N	(Re-submit) The corolla of the plant is obviously butterfly-shaped, with very distinct characteristics of the Fabaceae Fabaceae subfamily. It can be directly judged from the appearance of the flower that it belongs to the Fabaceae Fabaceae subfamily. The leaves of the plant are all single leaves, simple, degenerate or missing. The leaves are simple leaves, without stipules or separate stipules. The stamens are free structures. The two teeth on the upper part of the sepals are not fused into a wide notched lip, and the three teeth on the lower part are very small and narrow. The bracteoles are large, rounded, and easy to fall off; the leaves are tiny and also easy to fall off. The pods are spherical, and the inner part of the ovary is separated by a longitudinal septum, or the surface of the pods has longitudinal grooves. After comparing with plantnet, it can be confirmed that this is a plant of the genus Mirbelia. The leaves of the plant are 2 to 10 mm long, with a small recurved tip at the top. The leaves are linear, sometimes oblong or narrowly ovate, and are 1 to 5 mm wide. The upper surface of the leaves has a distinct reticular texture. After comparing it with plantnet, I can confirm that it is Mirbelia rubiifolia. In addition, using Robinson Book to search under the genus Mirbelia in the leguminous family, I can find that there are only two plants, and the other plant is a slender, erect shrub, and 1m high, which is completely different from the plant I obtained, so it can be ruled out. So it can also be confirmed that it is Mirbelia rubiifolia.	2024	10	6	\N	\N	\N		tianzhou	2024-10-09T10:42:41Z	\N	HUMAN_OBSERVATION	\N	tianzhou	CB Council
